tel 1.0

# The words Soundness's documentation uses with a precise meaning. A term in brackets in prose —
# `[tactic]`, or `[tactics][tactic]` for an inflected form — shows its definition when pointed
# at, and leads to the page named by its `link`. doc/standards/style.md says when to add one.

define accruing
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#accumulating-failures
  definition
      Describes a [strategy], such as `validate`, which records each error and lets the work
      continue, then gathers every recorded error into one, instead of stopping at the first.

define armored
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/cryptography.md#pem
  definition
      Describes binary data, such as a key, a certificate or a signature, written as ASCII text,
      in Base64 between `-----BEGIN …-----` and `-----END …-----` lines, as PEM does, so that it
      can be copied as text.

define boxing
  definition
      Wrapping a primitive value, such as an `Int` or a `Double`, in an object on the heap, so
      that it can be used where an object is expected, at the cost of an allocation. An unboxed
      value is stored as the primitive itself.

define brand
  definition
      A marker in a value's type which records where the value came from, or a fact about it, as
      in `Ratel.Row of brand`, so that a row can be read only through the reader which it came
      from.

define capability
  link  /doc/philosophy/capture-checking.md
  definition
      A value which must be in scope for an operation to be allowed, such as `Online` for a
      network request. A method that needs one says so in its signature, so what a method may
      do is visible in its type.

define capture checking
  link  /doc/philosophy/capture-checking.md
  definition
      An experimental check of the Scala 3 compiler which tracks the [capabilities][capability]
      each value captures, so that one lent to a scope cannot escape it, for example by being
      stored or returned.

define carrier
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/openapi.md#media-types-and-their-carriers
  definition
      Something which carries what another thing describes: the Scala type which holds a body of
      a given media type, such as `Json` for `application/json`; or a thread which runs, in
      turn, the tasks handed to it, as in a pool.

define choice package
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  keyword policy import
  definition
      A package of alternative [givens][given] for one decision, such as `strategies`,
      `alphabets` or `httpBackends`. Importing one member by name makes that choice for
      everything in scope.

define chunk
  definition
      One of the pieces in which a stream delivers its data, such as a block of bytes. Code
      which reads a stream must allow for a boundary, such as the end of a line, falling between
      two chunks.

define cipher
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/cryptography.md
  definition
      An algorithm which encrypts and decrypts data with a key, such as AES or RSA. The type of
      a key names its cipher, so a key cannot be used with another.

define classloader
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/classpath.md#choosing-a-classloader
  definition
      The JVM object which finds and loads compiled classes and resources, from a classpath or
      elsewhere. Resources and services are looked up through the `Classloader` in scope.

define clock tick
  definition
      One step of a clock which drives repeated work, such as a frame of an animation or an event
      reporting progress. It is a different thing from a [tick] on the axis of a chart.

define command-line flag
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/cli.md#flags
  definition
      A named argument to a command-line program, written with a leading `--`, such as
      `--verbose` or `--log-level`, which may take a value.

define compiletime
  definition
      While the program is being compiled. A check made at compiletime rejects wrong code
      before it can run; a compiletime error is reported by the compiler.

define contextual value
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  keyword implicit
  definition
      A value the compiler finds in scope and passes to a method without it being written as
      an argument. One is declared with `given` and requested with `using`.

define cooked mode
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/daemons.md#asking-for-a-cooked-terminal
  definition
      The ordinary mode of a terminal, in which input is delivered a line at a time, with echo
      and with line editing, such as Backspace, handled by the terminal driver. Its opposite,
      raw mode, delivers each keypress as it is typed.

define daemon
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/daemons.md
  definition
      A program which keeps running in the background and serves each invocation of a command,
      received from a small launcher, so that the command starts at once. In concurrency, a
      `daemon` is a task which is started and left to run until its scope ends.

define demangle
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/stack-traces.md#demangling
  definition
      To turn a name as the JVM sees it, encoded by the Scala compiler, such as `Foo$` or
      `$anonfun$1`, back into the name which was written in the source.

define derivation
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/derivation.md
  definition
      Having the compiler construct a [typeclass] instance for a case class or enumeration
      from the instances for its fields or cases, instead of writing it by hand.

define digest
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/hashing.md
  definition
      The fixed-length value which a hash algorithm, such as SHA-256, computes from any data or
      any value. Its type names the algorithm, and equal inputs always give equal digests.

define direct style
  link  /doc/philosophy/direct-style.md
  definition
      Code written as ordinary statements and expressions whose values are used directly,
      without wrapping results in a monad such as `Either`, `Try` or `Future`.

define endpoint
  definition
      The address at which a service is reached, and which a client connects to, such as a host
      and a port, a URL, or a command to start.

define enumeration
  definition
      A type declared with `enum` whose cases are a fixed list of values, known to the
      compiler, each with an ordinal giving its position in the declaration.

define evidence
  definition
      A [contextual value] whose presence proves something which the compiler cannot otherwise
      know, such as that one type is a subtype of another, or that a tool is available, and
      which a method demands before it can be called.

define face
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/fonts.md#typefaces-and-faces
  definition
      One style of a [typeface], a `Face`, chosen by its weight, slant and features, such as
      Inter in bold italic.

define fan-out
  definition
      Distributing work or data from one source to many recipients, such as a batch of small
      tasks spread across threads, or one stream delivered to several consumers.

define fingerprint
  definition
      A short value computed from some content, such as a schema or a key, which identifies it:
      comparing fingerprints confirms that two parties hold the same content without comparing
      the content itself.

define fire-and-forget
  definition
      Describes work which is started without waiting for it to finish or for any response, such
      as a notification in RPC, a datagram, or a task spawned with `daemon`.

define flag
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/flags.md
  definition
      A named yes-or-no setting, often stored as one bit of an integer, as in the flags field of
      a binary header. A set of flags is typed by an [enumeration], so a flag from another set
      is rejected.

define focus
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#saying-where-a-failure-was
  definition
      The position within a structure which code is currently working on, such as a JSON
      pointer, maintained by `track` as a traversal descends. An error recorded under `validate`
      is paired with the focus where it was raised.

define forwarder
  definition
      A method which does nothing but call another definition with the same arguments, such as
      one the compiler generates for an `export` clause, so that a member can be reached from
      somewhere else.

define frame
  definition
      One self-contained unit of a sequence: a message carved from a stream by [framing], a
      BinTEL document which travels on its own, or one image of an animation. An entry of a call
      stack is a [stack frame].

define framing
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/rpc.md#framing
  definition
      Carving a continuous stream of bytes or text into separate messages, by a length prefix, a
      `Content-Length` header or a line ending, so that each message can be read whole.

define frozen
  definition
      Made [immutable] once it has been built: a structure which is filled in by mutation and
      then frozen, such as a Bloom filter or a `Topology`, can be shared and queried, but no
      longer changed.

define getter
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/optics.md#lenses-as-values
  definition
      A function or method which reads one part of a value, such as a field. Paired with a
      [setter], it makes a lens.

define given
  link  /doc/philosophy/declarative-context.md
  definition
      A definition marked `given`, which makes a [contextual value] available to any code
      that has it in scope.

define grant
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/filesystem.md#reading-and-writing
  definition
      One of the kinds of access a [handle] is opened with, such as `Read`, `Write` or
      `Exclusive`. Grants are carried in the handle's type, so an operation which was not
      granted does not compile.

define grapheme cluster
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/characters.md#grapheme-clusters
  definition
      A sequence of one or more Unicode characters which a reader perceives as a single
      character, such as a letter with combining accents, or an emoji built from several code
      points.

define handle
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/filesystem.md#the-opening-pattern-generally
  definition
      A value which gives access to an open resource, such as a file, a canvas or a repository,
      for the duration of a scope. Its type says which operations it was opened for, and the
      resource is closed when the scope ends.

define handler
  definition
      A function supplied to be run when something arrives or happens, such as a request
      reaching a server or a message from a peer. It decides what is done with it and, for a
      request, what response is returned.

define harness
  definition
      The code which runs other code under controlled conditions and observes it, such as the
      `Harness` which runs a test and records what it captures, or a benchmark harness.

define honest signature
  link  /doc/philosophy/honest-signatures.md
  definition
      A method signature that states everything the method needs and every way it can fail,
      so that nothing about calling it is left to documentation.

define immutable
  link  /doc/philosophy/immutability.md
  definition
      Never changing after construction. An operation that appears to alter an immutable
      value returns a new value and leaves the original as it was.

define impossible state
  link  /doc/philosophy/impossible-states.md
  definition
      A value that would be meaningless or invalid, and which the types are designed to make
      unrepresentable, so that no code needs to check for it.

define interning
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/database.md#interning
  definition
      Storing each distinct value only once, so that storing or creating an equal value again
      gives the same instance or reference, and equal values can be compared by identity.

define interpolator
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/interpolation.md
  definition
      A prefix on a string literal, such as `t"…"`, `url"…"` or `media"…"`, which checks the
      literal's content at [compiletime] and gives a value of a precise type.

define keypress
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/terminal.md#keyboard-input
  definition
      One key, or combination of keys, pressed at a terminal, which is received as a `Keypress`
      value, such as a character, Enter or an arrow key.

define label
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/label-placement.md
  definition
      A piece of text placed beside the thing it names, such as a point on a chart or a town on
      a map; more generally, the short name a value is shown by, such as the label of a PEM
      block.

define layout
  definition
      The arrangement of parts in space or in memory: how a table, a terminal interface or a
      chart places its parts, or how the bits of a pixel are allocated among its channels.

define lenient
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/decoding.md#policies-for-optional-fields
  definition
      Describes a decoding policy which reads a missing or malformed optional value as `Unset`
      and reports nothing, instead of raising an error as a strict policy does.

define lift
  definition
      To carry a value into a richer setting which represents it, such as a value into a
      container, a constant into a quoted expression for a macro, or a fact about a value into
      its type.

define listener
  definition
      A value or task which waits for events or connections and acts on each as it arrives, such
      as the `Lsp.Listener` which receives what a language server sends unbidden, or a server
      waiting for connections on a socket.

define logger
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/logging.md
  definition
      The [contextual value] which receives the typed events that a method logs, and writes them
      somewhere, such as a terminal, a file or the system log. With no logger in scope, logging
      is silent and costs nothing.

define media type
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/media-types.md
  definition
      The standard name for the format of some data, such as `text/html` or `application/json`,
      which accompanies it in an HTTP header or an email, so that the receiver knows how to read
      it.

define one-shot
  definition
      Usable, or done, only once: a one-shot iterator can be traversed only once, and a one-shot
      operation, such as `gzip` on `Data`, processes a whole value in one call rather than
      incrementally.

define opaque type
  definition
      A type that is represented at runtime by another type, such as a `Long`, but which the
      compiler treats as distinct from it, so only the operations defined for it are allowed.

define optional
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/optional-values.md
  definition
      Typed `Optional[T]`: either a value of type `T`, or `Unset`. A present value is not
      wrapped, so it costs nothing more than the value itself.

define padding
  definition
      Characters or bytes added to a value to bring it to a required length: the spaces which
      fill text out to the width of a column, or the bytes which a block cipher appends to fill
      its last block.

define pane
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/terminal.md#layout
  definition
      A rectangular region of a terminal interface which shows one piece of content. Panes are
      nested into a tree, which is solved against the size of the terminal.

define park
  definition
      To suspend a thread or [strand], without consuming processor time, until another unparks
      it, as a writer does when its reader falls behind.

define pattern match
  definition
      Testing a value, in a `match` expression or a pattern elsewhere, against the shape of each
      case in turn, and extracting parts of the first one which fits.

define payload
  definition
      The data which a message or request carries, as distinct from the envelope around it, such
      as its method name, its identifier or its headers.

define permit
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/cryptography.md#weak-algorithms
  definition
      A [contextual value] which must be imported before a weak or deprecated algorithm, such as
      SHA-1, can be used, so that a codebase's acceptance of it is one import, which can be
      searched for.

define probate
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/concurrency.md#cancellation-in-both-directions
  definition
      The [contextual value] which decides what happens to a child task that is still running
      when the body of its parent completes: whether it is canceled, waited for, or treated as
      an error.

define provision
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/fonts.md#providing-a-typeface
  definition
      A [given] instance of `Typesettable` for a [typeface] in a medium, which says how that
      medium obtains the font and which faces it can then set. A font of the typeface exists
      only where its provision is in scope.

define raises
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#declaring-that-code-can-fail
  definition
      The infix type in a return type, as in `Text raises ParseError`, which declares that a
      method can fail with that error. It requires a [tactic] for the error from the caller.

define reason
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#defining-an-error
  definition
      The part of an error which says why it happened, usually a case of an [enumeration], such
      as `Json.Error.Reason.Absent`, so that code can tell one cause from another without
      reading a message.

define receiver
  definition
      The value on which a method is called, such as `x` in `x.f(y)`; also, in a protocol, the
      party which receives a message.

define remote
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/git.md#remotes-configuration-notes-and-the-reflog
  definition
      Another copy of a Git repository, named in the local one, such as `origin`, which commits
      are fetched from and pushed to.

define runner
  definition
      A program or value which runs code supplied by another, such as a test runner which runs
      the tests of a suite, or the launcher which runs as the client of a daemon.

define runtime
  definition
      While the program is running. A runtime error is one that the compiler could not rule
      out, and which is reported through a [tactic] when it happens.

define scratch
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/filesystem.md#creating-as-a-scope
  definition
      Describes a file or directory which is created for the duration of a scope, and removed
      when the scope ends, whether it succeeds or fails, as for a throwaway index or a test
      fixture.

define scrutinee
  definition
      The value being examined by a `match` expression or an extractor, which the pattern of
      each case is tested against.

define seam
  link  /doc/philosophy/decoupling.md
  definition
      The narrow point where one part of a system meets another which can be replaced, such as
      the few primitive operations through which a library reaches the platform beneath it. A
      different platform is supported by implementing the seam alone.

define session
  definition
      A scope which opens a resource, such as a connection, lends it to the code inside the
      scope, and closes it when the scope ends.

define setter
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/optics.md#lenses-as-values
  definition
      A function or method which replaces one part of a value, such as a field. For an
      [immutable] value it returns a new value with that part replaced. Paired with a [getter],
      it makes a lens.

define singleton type
  definition
      The type of exactly one value, such as the type of a particular [enumeration] case. A
      parameter of singleton type must be given a value the compiler can identify, not one
      computed at [runtime].

define sink
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/streams.md#writing-to-a-sink
  definition
      A destination which a stream of data is written to, such as a file, a socket or the input
      of a process; the counterpart of a source. A log sink is where a logger writes its events.

define span
  definition
      A contiguous stretch of something, given by where it starts and where it ends: a fragment
      of inline code in Markdown, a cell extending over several columns, or a range of a
      sequence.

define stack frame
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/stack-traces.md#resolving-frames-to-their-source
  definition
      One entry of a [stack trace]: a method which was executing, with its class, and the file
      and line it had reached.

define stack trace
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/stack-traces.md
  definition
      The list of method calls which were in progress at one moment, innermost first, as
      recorded when an exception or error is created. Each entry is a [stack frame].

define staging
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/staging.md
  definition
      Running a program in stages, where code quoted as `'{…}` in one stage is compiled and run
      in a later one. Soundness generalizes it, so that quoted code can run in another
      environment, such as a fresh JVM, and return its result.

define strand
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/concurrency.md#suspension-as-an-effect
  definition
      The unit of execution beneath a task: an abstraction of a thread with the four operations
      which suspension needs, which are interrupt, join, park and unpark. A supervisor supplies
      strands, so a scheduler which is not built on threads can be plugged in.

define strategy
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#choosing-a-strategy
  keyword error handling
  definition
      A choice, in scope, of what happens when an operation fails: `throwUnsafely` throws an
      exception, `safely` gives `Unset`, `capture` returns the error. A strategy supplies the
      [tactic] a fallible method asks for.

define summon
  definition
      To ask the compiler for the [contextual value] of a type which is in scope, by writing
      `summon[T]`, instead of receiving it as a parameter.

define swatch
  definition
      A small sample of a color, such as the square beside the name of a series in the legend of
      a chart.

define tactic
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/errors.md#declaring-that-code-can-fail
  definition
      A [contextual value] of type `Tactic[E]` which a method needs in order to report an
      error of type `E`. Whoever provides the tactic decides how the failure is handled.

define tag
  definition
      A short marker attached to a value to say what kind of value it is or what it is called,
      such as the name of an HTML element, the type of a YAML node or of an ASN.1 value, or the
      name given to a Git commit.

define tagged union
  definition
      A type whose values are each one of several cases, with a [tag] saying which case it is,
      such as a sealed trait or an enumeration, or a JSON object with a field naming its case.

define text wrapping
  definition
      Breaking text into lines which fit a given width, at spaces or at permitted hyphenation
      points, as when text is set in a terminal or a table.

define tick
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/charts.md#style
  definition
      A short mark on the axis of a chart at a regular value, usually with a label which gives
      that value.

define timeline
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/time.md
  definition
      A physical scale of time, such as Unix time or TAI, on which an instant is placed. The
      type of an instant names its timeline, as in `Instant over Unix`.

define timespan
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/time.md#durations-and-timespans
  definition
      A quantity of calendar time, in years, months, weeks, days, hours and minutes, whose
      length in seconds depends on when it is applied. A `Duration`, by contrast, is a fixed
      number of seconds.

define tokenizer
  definition
      The first stage of parsing, which splits source text into tokens, such as keywords,
      identifiers, literals and punctuation, before their structure is analyzed.

define toolchain
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/compiler.md
  definition
      The set of tools which together turn source code into something which can run, such as a
      compiler, a linker and a packager, with the versions and settings they are run with.

define trailer
  definition
      Data which comes after the main content of a message or file, rather than before it, such
      as the HTTP trailers sent after the body of a response, or the structure at the end of a
      ZIP archive or a PDF file.

define typeclass
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/derivation.md#products-sums-and-typeclasses
  definition
      A trait describing an ability a type may have, such as being shown, encoded or
      compared, with an instance for each type that has it. An instance is found as a
      [contextual value], so a type gains an ability without being modified. `Color is
      Showable` is the instance making `Color` showable.

define typeface
  link  /doc/soundness/modules/fonts.md#typefaces-and-faces
  definition
      A font family, such as Inter, named by a `Typeface`, independently of any weight, slant or
      file. A [face] chooses one style from it.

define variant
  definition
      One of several alternative forms of the same thing, such as the URL-safe alphabet of
      Base64, a dialect of delimited text, or one alternative of a TEL select.

define viewport
  definition
      The rectangle in which something is shown, such as the visible area of a browser window,
      which CSS units like `Vw` are fractions of, or the area an SVG image is drawn in.

define wire form
  definition
      The form a value takes when it is sent between programs, in a protocol or a serialized
      format, as distinct from its form in Scala: the name a field is sent under, the code an
      error is sent as, or the bytes of its encoding.
